was 20 and playing for Dortmund when three pipe bombs went off right next t=
o our bus on the way to a Champions League match. A lot of young people pro=
bably would not even believe that it happened, or how bad it was, but it ma=
rked us all. There was a deafening noise, and then I remember the bus nearl=
y tipping over. I looked behind me and saw blood on the floor. Smoke, screa=
ms. I remember being at the hospital later that night, waiting for Marc Bar=
tra, who had his wrist badly cut up. The next day, someone told me that a h=
uge piece of shrapnel was lodged in the headrest of the seat next to Marc. =
My usual seat. That day, the seat was empty. My legs were feeling a bit tig=
ht, and the seats in the front reclined. So I sat up there instead, and it =
may have saved my life=E2=80=9D =E2=80=93 Mikel Merino recalls how a last-m=
inute change of seats on the Borussia Dortmund coach that was hit by explos=
ives on its way to the home leg of their Champions League semi-final agains=
t Monaco in 2017 may have saved his life in a wider interview with the Play=
er Tribune.</p></div></td></tr>
